    id: "time",
    title: "Clock time, days and routine",
    blurb: "Ask and tell the time, say when something happens, name the days, and describe your daily routine.",
    deck: "Time, days and routine",
    body: `
<p>To ask the time, Kannada asks "how much is the hour?": <b>ಗಂಟೆ ಎಷ್ಟು?</b> <i>gaṇṭe eshṭu?</i>. The answer is a number, written as a word, plus <b>ಗಂಟೆ</b> <i>gaṇṭe</i> (hour, o'clock).</p>
{{ex:ಗಂಟೆ ಎಷ್ಟು?|gaṇṭe eshṭu?|What time is it?}}
{{ex:ಈಗ ಐದು ಗಂಟೆ.|īga aidu gaṇṭe.|It's five o'clock now.}}
<h3>Half past: -ಊವರೆ</h3>
<p><b>ವರೆ</b> <i>vare</i> means "and a half". The number's last -ಉ becomes a long -ಊ and joins it, all as one word: <b>ಏಳು</b> → <b>ಏಳೂವರೆ</b> <i>ēḷūvare</i> (7:30), <b>ಎರಡು</b> → <b>ಎರಡೂವರೆ</b> (2:30), <b>ಹತ್ತು</b> → <b>ಹತ್ತೂವರೆ</b> (10:30). Clocks and tickets show digits; say the Kannada words.</p>
{{ex:ಈಗ ಎರಡೂವರೆ.|īga eraḍūvare.|It's two thirty now.}}
<p>The same half works away from the clock: <b>ಒಂದೂವರೆ</b> is "one and a half" and <b>ಎರಡೂವರೆ</b> "two and a half", for hours, kilos or liters.</p>
<h3>"At" a time: -ಗೆ after the noun</h3>
<p>English puts "at" in front: "at seven". Kannada puts the "to" ending -ಗೆ after the time: <b>ಏಳು ಗಂಟೆಗೆ</b> <i>ēḷu gaṇṭege</i>, <b>ಏಳೂವರೆಗೆ</b>. To ask "at what time?", say <b>ಎಷ್ಟು ಗಂಟೆಗೆ?</b></p>
{{ex:ನಾನು ಒಂಬತ್ತು ಗಂಟೆಗೆ ಆಫೀಸಿಗೆ ಹೋಗ್ತೀನಿ.|nānu ombattu gaṇṭege āfīsige hōgtīni.|I go to the office at nine.}}
{{ex:ನೀವು ಎಷ್ಟು ಗಂಟೆಗೆ ಬರ್ತೀರಿ?|nīvu eshṭu gaṇṭege bartīri?|What time will you come?}}
<h3>Parts of the day</h3>
<p><b>ಬೆಳಿಗ್ಗೆ</b> <i>beḷigge</i> (morning), <b>ಮಧ್ಯಾಹ್ನ</b> <i>madhyāhna</i> (afternoon; usually said madhyāna), <b>ಸಂಜೆ</b> <i>sanje</i> (evening) and <b>ರಾತ್ರಿ</b> <i>rātri</i> (night) need no ending. They come before the clock time: <b>ಬೆಳಿಗ್ಗೆ ಏಳು ಗಂಟೆಗೆ</b>, "at seven in the morning".</p>
{{ex:ಅವಳು ಸಂಜೆ ಆರು ಗಂಟೆಗೆ ಮನೆಗೆ ಬರ್ತಾಳೆ.|avaḷu sanje āru gaṇṭege manege bartāḷe.|She comes home at six in the evening.}}
<h3>Days of the week</h3>
<p>Every day name ends in <b>ವಾರ</b>, which also means "week". "On Monday" needs no extra word: just the day.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Kannada</th><th>English</th></tr><tr><td class="k">ಸೋಮವಾರ</td><td>Monday</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ಮಂಗಳವಾರ</td><td>Tuesday</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ಬುಧವಾರ</td><td>Wednesday</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ಗುರುವಾರ</td><td>Thursday</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ಶುಕ್ರವಾರ</td><td>Friday</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ಶನಿವಾರ</td><td>Saturday</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ಭಾನುವಾರ</td><td>Sunday</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:ಭಾನುವಾರ ನಾನು ಕೆಲಸ ಮಾಡಲ್ಲ.|bhānuvāra nānu kelasa māḍalla.|I don't work on Sunday.}}
<h3>A day in your life</h3>
<p>Routines use the present-future from the last two lessons, with the person ending at the end: <b>ನಾನು ರಾತ್ರಿ ಹನ್ನೊಂದು ಗಂಟೆಗೆ ಮಲಗ್ತೀನಿ</b>, "I go to bed at eleven at night" (<b>ಮಲಗು</b> <i>malagu</i>, sleep). A routine is a chain of such sentences: leaving for work, coming home, dinner, bed. The time phrase usually sits right after the person, and the verb with its ending closes each sentence. Quarter hours have their own words; this course leaves them for later.</p>
<p>The romanisation is a crutch: read the Kannada first and glance down only to check.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> leaving out the ending for "at". <b>ನಾನು ಏಳು ಗಂಟೆ ಬರ್ತೀನಿ</b> → <b>ನಾನು ಏಳು ಗಂಟೆಗೆ ಬರ್ತೀನಿ</b>.</div>
`,
